<section xml:id="section-mass-center-of-mass" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Applications: Mass and Center of Mass</title>

<introduction>
<p>
Multiple integrals let us compute physical quantities of solid objects
and flat plates whose <term>density</term> varies from point to point.
In this section we use double and triple integrals to compute the total
mass of an object and to locate its <term>center of mass</term><mdash/>the
point at which the object would balance perfectly.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-mass-3d-object">
<title>Finding the Center of Mass of a 3D Object</title>

<p>
Suppose a solid object occupies a region <m>D</m> in space and its
density (mass per unit volume) at each point is given by a continuous
function <m>\delta(x,y,z)</m>. To measure the total mass, we partition
<m>D</m> into small boxes, as in <xref ref="fig-mass-element-tikz"/>.
If the <m>k</m>-th box contains the point <m>(x_k, y_k, z_k)</m> and has
volume <m>\Delta V_k</m>, write down its approximate mass, sum over the
boxes, and let the size of the boxes shrink to zero.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,5.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the total mass as a triple integral.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-mass-3d">
<title>Mass of a Solid</title>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>
\amp M = \lim_{n \to \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{n}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad \delta(x_k, y_k, z_k)\, \Delta V_k
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-mass-3d" number="yes">
\amp = \iiint_D \delta(x,y,z)\, dV.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
Watch the construction unfold in <xref ref="fig-mass-element-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-mass-element-video">
<caption>
Partitioning a solid <m>D</m> into small boxes: each box carries the
mass <m>\Delta m_k = \delta(x_k,y_k,z_k)\,\Delta V_k</m>, and the
Riemann sum of these masses converges to the triple integral of the
density.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-mass-element"
       source="videos/mass_element_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-mass-element-tikz">
<caption>
A solid region <m>D</m> with a small box around the point
<m>(x_k, y_k, z_k)</m>. The box has volume <m>\Delta V_k</m> and mass
<m>\Delta m_k = \delta(x_k, y_k, z_k)\, \Delta V_k</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-mass-element-tikz" width="75%">
<shortdescription>
A potato-shaped solid in space with a small highlighted box inside it.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A translucent potato-shaped solid labeled <m>D</m> floats above the
<m>xy</m>-plane in a three-dimensional coordinate system. A small
purple box inside the solid marks the point <m>(x_k, y_k, z_k)</m>,
and a leader line labels its mass
<m>\Delta m_k = \delta(x_k, y_k, z_k)\, \Delta V_k</m>.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  \tikzset{lbl/.style={fill=white, fill opacity=0.82, text opacity=1, inner sep=2.5pt, rounded corners=2pt}}
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  \definecolor{oipurple}{RGB}{204,121,167}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{18},
      width=12cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis equal image,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=-2.9, xmax=2.9, ymin=-2.9, ymax=2.9, zmin=0, zmax=4.1,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- the solid D: a deformed (potato-like) ellipsoid ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=interp,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softg}{color=(oigreen!14!white) color=(oigreen!48!white)},
              opacity=0.55, fill opacity=0.55,
              domain=0:360, samples=49,
              y domain=0:180, samples y=25,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({(2+0.35*sin(2*x))*cos(x)*sin(y)},
       {(1.35+0.2*cos(2*x))*sin(x)*sin(y)},
       {0.95*cos(y)+2.3});
    % ---- small box (mass element) inside the solid ----
    % cube centered near (1.05, 0.55, 2.6) with half-side 0.21
    \def\cx{1.05}\def\cy{0.55}\def\cz{2.60}\def\hs{0.21}
    % top face
    \fill[oipurple!70, draw=oipurple!35!black, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx-\hs},{\cy-\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy-\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx-\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) -- cycle;
    % front face (toward viewer, +x side)
    \fill[oipurple!45, draw=oipurple!35!black, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy-\hs},{\cz-\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz-\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy-\hs},{\cz+\hs}) -- cycle;
    % side face (+y side)
    \fill[oipurple!30, draw=oipurple!35!black, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx-\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz-\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz-\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx-\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) -- cycle;
    % ---- labels ----
    \node[lbl, font=\large] at (axis cs:-1.7,-0.4,3.1) {$D$};
    \draw[black!70, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) -- (axis cs:1.6,2.0,3.7);
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize, anchor=west] at (axis cs:1.6,2.0,3.7)
      {$\Delta m_k = \delta(x_k,y_k,z_k)\,\Delta V_k$};
    \draw[black!70, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx},{\cy+\hs},{\cz-\hs}) -- (axis cs:1.2,2.3,1.9);
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize, anchor=west] at (axis cs:1.2,2.3,1.9)
      {$(x_k, y_k, z_k)$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The same idea locates the balance point of the object. Each small box
contributes a <term>moment</term> about each coordinate plane<mdash/>its
mass times its (signed) distance from that plane. Adding these
contributions and passing to the limit gives the
<term>first moments</term>
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
M_{yz} \amp = \iiint_D x\, \delta\, dV,
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-first-moments-3d" number="yes">
M_{xz} \amp = \iiint_D y\, \delta\, dV,
</mrow>
<mrow>
M_{xy} \amp = \iiint_D z\, \delta\, dV.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
The <term>center of mass</term> <m>(\bar{x}, \bar{y}, \bar{z})</m> is
the point whose coordinates are the moments divided by the total mass:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\bar{x} \amp = \frac{M_{yz}}{M},
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-com-3d" number="yes">
\bar{y} \amp = \frac{M_{xz}}{M},
</mrow>
<mrow>
\bar{z} \amp = \frac{M_{xy}}{M}.
</mrow>
</md>

<example xml:id="example-paraboloid-com">
<title>Center of Mass of a Solid Bounded by a Paraboloid</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the center of mass of a solid of constant density <m>\delta</m>,
which is bounded by the disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m> (in the plane
<m>z = 0</m>) and the paraboloid <m>z = 4 - x^2 - y^2</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,16);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,15.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the example Center of Mass of a Solid Bounded by a Paraboloid.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-paraboloid-com-video">
<caption>
The solid bounded above by <m>z = 4 - x^2 - y^2</m> and below by the
disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m>. By symmetry the center of mass lies on the
<m>z</m>-axis; use the animation to check its height.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-paraboloid-com"
       source="videos/paraboloid_com_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-paraboloid-com-tikz">
<caption>
The solid of constant density bounded by the paraboloid
<m>z = 4 - x^2 - y^2</m> and the disk <m>R</m>: <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m>
in the plane <m>z = 0</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-paraboloid-com-tikz" width="80%">
<shortdescription>
A dome-shaped solid over a disk.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
The paraboloid <m>z = 4 - x^2 - y^2</m> forms a translucent green dome
over the disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane, labeled
<m>R</m>. The dashed <m>z</m>-axis passes through the apex at height 4.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  \tikzset{lbl/.style={fill=white, fill opacity=0.82, text opacity=1, inner sep=2.5pt, rounded corners=2pt}}
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oimagenta}{RGB}{204,0,119}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{18},
      width=12.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis equal image,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=-3, xmax=4.4, ymin=-3, ymax=3, zmin=0, zmax=4.6,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- base disk R ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=flat,
              color=black!12, opacity=0.4, fill opacity=0.4,
              domain=0:360, samples=37,
              y domain=0:2, samples y=2,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({y*cos(x)}, {y*sin(x)}, {0});
    % ---- paraboloid surface ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=interp,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softg}{color=(oigreen!14!white) color=(oigreen!48!white)},
              opacity=0.55, fill opacity=0.55,
              domain=0:360, samples=61,
              y domain=0:2, samples y=25,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({y*cos(x)}, {y*sin(x)}, {4 - y^2});
    % light parallels for shape cues
    \foreach \rr in {0.5,1,1.5}{
      \edef\tmp{\noexpand\addplot3[black!22, line width=0.35pt, domain=0:360,
          samples=73, samples y=0, forget plot]
        ({\rr*cos(x)}, {\rr*sin(x)}, {4 - \rr*\rr});}\tmp}
    % light meridians for shape cues
    \foreach \uu in {0,45,...,315}{
      \edef\tmp{\noexpand\addplot3[black!22, line width=0.35pt, domain=0:2,
          samples=25, samples y=0, forget plot]
        ({x*cos(\uu)}, {x*sin(\uu)}, {4 - x^2});}\tmp}
    % ---- boundary circle x^2 + y^2 = 4 ----
    \addplot3[line width=1.6pt, oiblue, domain=0:360, samples=121,
        samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({2*cos(x)}, {2*sin(x)}, {0});
    % ---- dashed axis of symmetry through the solid ----
    \draw[dashed, black!60, line width=0.7pt]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,0,4);
    % ---- labels ----
    \draw[black!70, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{1.35*cos(35)},{1.35*sin(35)},{4-1.35^2}) -- (axis cs:1.9,2.5,3.4);
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize, anchor=west] at (axis cs:1.9,2.5,3.4)
      {$z = 4 - x^2 - y^2$};
    \draw[black!70, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{2*cos(320)},{2*sin(320)},0) -- (axis cs:1.4,-2.75,0.75);
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize, anchor=east] at (axis cs:1.4,-2.75,0.75)
      {$x^2 + y^2 = 4$};
    \node[lbl, font=\large, anchor=north west] at (axis cs:2.0,1.3,0) {$R$};
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize] at (axis cs:-0.3,-0.25,0) {$0$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-centroid">
<title>Centroids</title>

<definition xml:id="def-centroid">
<statement>
<p>
When the density of a solid object (as in the previous example) or plate
(as in the next example) is constant, the center of mass is called the
<term>centroid</term> of the object.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
As we saw in <xref ref="example-paraboloid-com"/>, a constant density
<m>\delta</m> cancels in the quotients of
<xref ref="eq-com-3d"/>, so the centroid depends only on the shape of
the object. For a flat plate occupying a region <m>R</m> in the plane
with density <m>\delta(x,y)</m> (mass per unit area), the same
reasoning that gave <xref ref="eq-mass-3d"/> and
<xref ref="eq-first-moments-3d"/><mdash/>with double integrals in place
of triple integrals<mdash/>gives the mass and first moments
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
M \amp = \iint_R \delta\, dA,
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-first-moments-2d" number="yes">
M_y \amp = \iint_R x\, \delta\, dA,
</mrow>
<mrow>
M_x \amp = \iint_R y\, \delta\, dA,
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
where <m>M_y</m> is the moment about the <m>y</m>-axis and <m>M_x</m>
the moment about the <m>x</m>-axis, and the center of mass is
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-com-2d">
\bar{x} = \frac{M_y}{M}, \qquad
\bar{y} = \frac{M_x}{M}.
</men>

<example xml:id="example-centroid-between-curves">
<title>Centroid of the Region Between Two Curves</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the centroid of the region between <m>y = \sqrt{x}</m> and
<m>y = x^2</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,16);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,15.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the example Centroid of the Region Between Two Curves.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-centroid-between-curves-video">
<caption>
The region between <m>y = \sqrt{x}</m> and <m>y = x^2</m> is symmetric
about the line <m>y = x</m>, and its centroid lies on that line. Use the
animation to check its coordinates.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-centroid-between-curves"
       source="videos/centroid_between_curves_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-centroid-between-curves">
<caption>
The region between <m>y = \sqrt{x}</m> (blue) and <m>y = x^2</m> (red)
over <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>
The region between the square-root curve and the parabola, from 0 to 1.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-centroid-between-curves">
<diagram dimensions="(320,320)" margins="10">
<definition>f(x) = sqrt(x)</definition>
<definition>g(x) = x^2</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.35,-0.35,1.55,1.55)">
<grid-axes at="cb-grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<area-between-curves at="cb-region" functions="(f,g)" domain="(0,1)"
                     fill="#9ecfff" stroke="none"/>
<graph at="cb-sqrt" function="f" domain="(0,1.5)" stroke="blue"
       thickness="2.5"/>
<graph at="cb-square" function="g" domain="(-0.2,1.22)" stroke="red"
       thickness="2.5"/>
<point at="cb-origin" p="(0,0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
<point at="cb-one" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(1,1)" fill="black" size="3" alignment="se">
<m>(1,1)</m>
</point>
<label at="cb-sqrt-label" anchor="(1.5,0.72)" alignment="sw"
       color="blue">
<m>y=\sqrt{x}</m>
</label>
<label at="cb-square-label" anchor="(1.1,1.4)" alignment="w"
       color="red">
<m>y=x^2</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate grid showing the region between the curves y equals the square root of x, in blue, and y equals x squared, in red. The curves intersect at the origin and at the point 1 comma 1, enclosing a lens-shaped region shaded light blue.">
  <annotation ref="cb-sqrt" text="The blue curve y equals the square root of x, the upper boundary of the region."/>
  <annotation ref="cb-square" text="The red curve y equals x squared, the lower boundary of the region."/>
  <annotation ref="cb-region" text="The lens-shaped region between the two curves, shaded light blue, lying over the interval from 0 to 1."/>
  <annotation ref="cb-one" text="The point 1 comma 1, where the two curves intersect."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-cardioid-centroid">
<title>Centroid of a Cardioid</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the centroid of the object that has the shape
<m>r = 1 + \cos(\theta)</m> and density <m>\delta = 2</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,14.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the example Centroid of a Cardioid.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-cardioid-centroid-video">
<caption>
The cardioid <m>r = 1 + \cos(\theta)</m> with constant density: symmetry
places the centroid on the <m>x</m>-axis, and the polar moment integrals
locate it. Use the animation to check your value.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-cardioid-centroid"
       source="videos/cardioid_centroid_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-cardioid-centroid">
<caption>
The cardioid <m>r = 1 + \cos(\theta)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>
A cardioid symmetric about the x axis with shaded interior.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cardioid-centroid">
<diagram dimensions="(360,300)" margins="10">
<definition>c(t) = ((1 + cos(t))*cos(t), (1 + cos(t))*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.9,-1.55,2.65,1.55)">
<axes at="cd-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="cd-cardioid" function="c" domain="(0,2*pi)"
                  closed="yes" fill="#9ecfff" stroke="blue"
                  thickness="2.5"/>
<label at="cd-curve-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(1.55,-1.05)" alignment="se"
       color="blue">
<m>r=1+\cos(\theta)</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A cardioid, the heart-shaped curve r equals 1 plus cosine theta, drawn in blue with its interior shaded light blue. It has a cusp at the origin and its widest point at x equals 2.">
  <annotation ref="cd-cardioid" text="The cardioid r equals 1 plus cosine theta, a closed heart-shaped curve with a cusp at the origin, shaded light blue."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

</section>
